module bindings.javascript.static_file_request_path_component_decode;

// `decodeURI` and `decodeURIComponent` are not two spellings of one operation.
// MDN states the difference plainly: `decodeURI` leaves the escape sequences a
// URI's reserved delimiters would have to be written as — `%23`, `%2F`, `%3F`,
// `%3B`, `%26`, `%3D` — encoded, because decoding them would change the
// structure of the URI; `decodeURIComponent` decodes every escape sequence,
// which is correct for one already-extracted component and wrong for a whole
// path. That is why the two exist.
//
// A static-file handler that builds the filesystem path from
// `decodeURIComponent(request path)` therefore turns `%2F` into a directory
// separator that was not there when the request was routed. The router matched
// the encoded spelling, so `/static/admin%2Fsecret.txt` is one path segment to
// route matching and to every middleware registered at `/static/admin/*`, and
// two segments to the handler, which opens `<root>/static/admin/secret.txt` and
// serves it. The middleware never ran. The same respelling reaches any file the
// handler can name, so an authentication, authorization or rate-limiting
// middleware scoped to a subtree of the served root is bypassed by any client
// that can reach the route. Parent-directory rejection does not address this:
// no `..` segment is involved.
//
// So the binding reads the decoder, the request path it is applied to, and the
// fact that the result is used to open a file under a root:
//
//   * `call_path:decodeURIComponent` — the whole-string decoder, the one that
//     decodes reserved delimiters. A handler that decodes with `decodeURI`, or
//     that decodes segment by segment after splitting on `/`, does not carry
//     this token and is not reported;
//   * a `req.path` / `req.url` / `ctx.path` / `url.pathname` selector — the
//     value being decoded is the request's own path rather than a query
//     parameter or a form field, which are components and are `decodeURIComponent`'s
//     documented use;
//   * a `join` or `resolve` — the decoded path is composed onto a serving root;
//   * and a filesystem read of the composed path.
//
// Nothing here names a remediation helper. The route out of the defect is to
// stop decoding reserved delimiters, which removes the first conjunct however
// the author spells it — a direct `decodeURI`, a wrapper around it, or a
// per-segment decode — so a fix is not recognised by its own vocabulary.
//
// Two costs. A JavaScript function context inherits the tokens of the functions
// nested inside it, so a handler returned from a factory reports on the factory
// as well as on the handler; the extra row points at the same defect. And a
// handler that reaches `decodeURIComponent` through a helper of its own keeps
// the token in the helper's context rather than its own, so that spelling is
// not reported.
binding staticFileRequestPathComponentDecode {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.callPath == "decodeURIComponent"
    and containsAny(node.context.selector, ["req.path", "req.url", "req.originalUrl", "request.path", "request.url", "ctx.path", "ctx.url", "url.pathname"])
    and node.context.callPath in ["join", "path.join", "resolve", "path.resolve"]
    and node.context.callPath in ["createReadStream", "fs.createReadStream", "readFile", "readFileSync", "fs.readFile", "fs.readFileSync", "statSync", "fs.statSync", "sendFile", "openSync", "fs.openSync"]
  emit issue code.MiddlewarePathMatchNormalizationMismatch at node
}
